INT. BONDED TEA WAREHOUSE - AFTER CLOSING

A cathedral of tea chests, stacked in towers to the rafters. Shafts of lamplight. The smell of bergamot and dust. A catwalk runs across the beams.

LEOPOLDINE "LEO" MARCHAND-ADEBISI, 40s, customs inspector, long coat, lanyard, walks the floor slowly, a clipboard against her hip. She stops at a chest labelled DARJEELING FIRST FLUSH. She taps it. Hollow.

A footstep behind her. She does not turn.

LEO
You're late, Anton.

ANTON KARPINSKI-OLUWALE, 50s, in a dark overcoat, emerges from between two towers of chests. He carries a small leather case.

ANTON
Traffic.

LEO
There was no traffic.

ANTON
Then I walked slowly.

She finally turns. He smiles, a thin, kind smile. She doesn't return it.

LEO
Open it.

ANTON
You said no names.

LEO
I said no names on the phone.

He sets the case on a chest. Unlatches it. Inside: tea. Real tea, loose, pale green. He takes a pinch and holds it out.

ANTON
Taste.

She takes it, rolls it between finger and thumb, sniffs. Her expression changes.

LEO
It's the Himalayan Silver Tip.

ANTON
It is.

LEO
That doesn't exist anymore.

ANTON
It exists in eleven chests.

LEO
In this warehouse?

ANTON
In this warehouse.

She puts the leaf in her mouth. Chews. Swallows. Closes her eyes.

LEO
It's extraordinary.

ANTON
It's illegal.

LEO
It's both.

A sound from the catwalk overhead. Both look up. A silhouette moves between the beams.

Leo draws a pistol from under her coat. Anton raises both hands.

ANTON (CONT'D)
Don't.

LEO
Who is that?

ANTON
My daughter.

LEO
You brought your daughter?

ANTON
She insisted.

The silhouette leans over the rail. A young woman, perhaps twenty, with Anton's eyes.

LEO
Come down.

She does, down a rope ladder, one slow rung at a time. At the bottom she lowers her hood. The lamplight catches her face.

ANTON
Ines.

INES KARPINSKI-OLUWALE, 20s, stands between her father and the inspector. Hands open.

INES
Dad, it's fine.

LEO
Both of you, step back.

They speak at once.

ANTON
Leo, put it down.

INES ^
Please, please, please.

Leo holds the gun steady for three long seconds. Then, slowly, she lowers it.

LEO
Eleven chests.

ANTON
Eleven.

LEO
I get five.

ANTON
Four.

LEO
Five, Anton. Or the daughter sings.

Ines glances at her father. He sighs.

ANTON
Five.

Leo holsters the gun. She lifts the clipboard. Writes something, tears off the page, hands it to him.

LEO
That's a receipt for the tea. As seized.

ANTON
(reading)
It says "destroyed."

LEO
It will be, by the end of the week.

She walks back into the stacks. Her footsteps fade. The lamp hums. Ines exhales for the first time in a minute.

INES
Dad.

ANTON
Yes?

INES
Next time I'll stay in the car.
